(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-neuro-ophthalmology-vignettes"]={"title":"Neuro-Ophthalmology Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 35-year-old man has a traumatic fourth nerve palsy with binocular diplopia. What is advised in the interim?","choices":["Arrange urgent angiography","Patch one eye","Immediate strabismus surgery","Start systemic corticosteroids","Prescribe pilocarpine drops"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, to relieve binocular diplopia while observing for about six months. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has painful vision loss in one eye and a relative afferent pupillary defect. Which test is most appropriate?","choices":["Tonometry","Temporal artery biopsy","Lumbar puncture first","MRI brain and orbits","Carotid Doppler"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with and without contrast, looking for demyelinating lesions. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has reduced vision in one eye. Which bedside manoeuvre detects a relative afferent pupillary defect?","choices":["Cover-uncover test","Dilute pilocarpine drops","Apraclonidine drops","Tonometry","Swinging flashlight test"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Moving the light to the affected eye makes both pupils dilate. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman develops new monocular nystagmus. Which is the most appropriate step?","choices":["Patch the affected eye","Prescribe prism glasses","Refer to ophthalmology","Reassure her it is physiologic","Start a calcium channel blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nystagmus acquired in adulthood, and monocular nystagmus, both warrant work-up. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has ptosis and miosis on the left. You want to confirm the diagnosis in clinic. Which test is used?","choices":["Tonometry","Swinging flashlight test","Dilute apraclonidine drops","Dilute pilocarpine drops","Fluorescein staining"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ineffective in a normal pupil, they dilate the Horner pupil in most patients. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man's pupils do not react to light but constrict normally when he focuses on a near object. What is this phenomenon called?","choices":["Light-near dissociation","Relative afferent pupillary defect","Dilation lag","Sector paralysis","Anisocoria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The near pathway skips part of the route the light pathway takes. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a left ptosis, a dilated left pupil and the left eye deviated down and out. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["Sixth","Seventh","Second","Third","Fourth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Levator, pupil and most extraocular muscles are all oculomotor. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is found drowsy with pinpoint pupils and a respiratory rate of 8. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Naloxone","Apraclonidine drops","Pilocarpine drops","Acetazolamide","Atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and repeat dosing may be needed because it lasts about two hours. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A nurse develops a unilateral fixed dilated pupil after handling a scopolamine patch. What should she be told?","choices":["This is early Horner syndrome","She should start pilocarpine drops","It will resolve as the drug wears off","She needs urgent angiography","This indicates an Adie pupil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pharmacologic mydriasis is transient and needs no imaging. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with a fourth nerve palsy asks why she keeps tilting her head. What is the best explanation?","choices":["It prevents the pupil dilating","It improves peripheral vision","Tilting compensates for the weak muscle","Tilting reduces intraocular pressure","It is an unrelated habit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She tilts away from the affected side to reduce the vertical deviation. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has an isolated, atraumatic sixth nerve palsy. Which investigation is indicated?","choices":["MRI brain with and without contrast","Stat computed tomography angiography","Lumbar puncture","Temporal artery biopsy","Carotid Doppler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and check haemoglobin A1C if there are risk factors and no known diabetes. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman is told she has an Adie tonic pupil. What is the most appropriate thing to tell her?","choices":["It is often benign and asymptomatic","It signals an intracranial aneurysm","It indicates tertiary syphilis","It will progress to blindness","It requires urgent vascular imaging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, though photophobia and blurred near vision can occur. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant is noted to have nystagmus. What is the next step?","choices":["Repeat examination at age five","Start patching immediately","Order a lumbar puncture","Refer for work-up","Reassure, as it is always physiologic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infants and young children with nystagmus warrant referral. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has sudden ptosis, a dilated unreactive left pupil, and the eye rests down and out. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassurance and review in six months","Temporal artery biopsy","Lumbar puncture","Stat computed tomography angiography","Routine outpatient imaging"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pupil-involved third nerve palsy is an aneurysm until proven otherwise. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has suspected Horner syndrome. Why does apraclonidine produce dilation in the affected eye only?","choices":["It paralyses accommodation","It stimulates the ciliary ganglion","The denervated pupil is supersensitive","It blocks the iris sphincter directly","It raises intraocular pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so a drop ineffective in a normal pupil dilates the affected one. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient has horizontal jerk nystagmus. How is jerk nystagmus named?","choices":["For the amplitude of the movement","For the direction of the fast beat","For the direction of the slow phase","For the eye that is affected","For the underlying cause"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it increases on gaze in that direction. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has bilateral small pupils with light-near dissociation. Which additional examination does that association call for?","choices":["Measure intraocular pressure","Palpate the temporal arteries","Auscultate the carotids","Check haemoglobin A1C only","Test posterior column function"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Tabes dorsalis with sensory ataxia accompanies it in tertiary syphilis. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a history of anterior uveitis is prescribed a nebulised bronchodilator. Which risk should she be warned about?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Central retinal artery occlusion","Papilledema","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Retinal detachment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nebulised bronchodilators and systemic anticholinergics are listed risk factors. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 43-year-old man has 0.3 mm of anisocoria, equal in light and dark, with normal lids and movements. What is the most appropriate step?","choices":["Stat angiography","Apraclonidine testing","MRI brain and orbits","Urgent ophthalmology referral","Reassurance"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. This is physiologic anisocoria and needs no work-up. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why may anhidrosis be absent in a patient with confirmed Horner syndrome?","choices":["It depends where the lesion sits","Anhidrosis only occurs in children","Sweating is parasympathetic","It requires bilateral lesions","It is only seen with tumours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The triad may be incomplete depending on the level along the pathway. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has acute Horner syndrome with neck pain after a chiropractic manipulation. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Apraclonidine drops in clinic","Reassurance and review in a month","Start aspirin and discharge","Refer routinely to ophthalmology","Urgent vascular imaging"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Carotid dissection must be identified before it embolises. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has a large right pupil. It constricts briskly to dilute pilocarpine. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adie tonic pupil","Pharmacologic mydriasis","Third nerve palsy","Horner syndrome","Argyll Robertson pupil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Denervation supersensitivity is what makes a dilute drop work. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has a unilateral large, poorly reactive pupil. Which bedside test supports an Adie pupil?","choices":["Gonioscopy","Dilute pilocarpine drops","Dilute apraclonidine drops","Tonometry","Fluorescein staining"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The denervated pupil is supersensitive and constricts. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has blurred vision in the right eye. On swinging a light from the left eye to the right, both pupils dilate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Horner syndrome","Adie tonic pupil","Third nerve palsy","Physiologic anisocoria","Relative afferent pupillary defect"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A Marcus Gunn pupil, meaning an afferent lesion at the retina or optic nerve. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with diabetes has a third nerve palsy with a reactive, normal-sized pupil. What is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Stat computed tomography angiography","No imaging at all","Immediate neurosurgical referral","Temporal artery biopsy","Imaging, but not stat"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Pupil sparing points to microvascular disease rather than an aneurysm. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with Argyll Robertson pupils asks what else to look for. Which associated finding should be sought?","choices":["Painful eye movement","Sensory ataxia","Jaw claudication","Curtain-like visual loss","Coloured halos around lights"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, from tabes dorsalis with posterior column involvement. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has fatigable bilateral ptosis and variable diplopia. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Apraclonidine drops","Patch one eye and review in six months","Dilute pilocarpine drops","Refer for neurologic evaluation","Stat computed tomography angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pattern points away from a structural palsy and toward myasthenia. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has small pupils bilaterally that do not constrict to light but constrict briskly when he looks at a near target. He also has sensory ataxia. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bilateral optic neuritis","Argyll Robertson pupil","Adie tonic pupil","Horner syndrome","Third nerve palsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Light-near dissociation with miosis, classically tertiary syphilis with tabes dorsalis. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has drooping of both lids that is mild in the morning and marked by evening, with intermittent double vision. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adie tonic pupil","Physiologic anisocoria","Myasthenia gravis","Horner syndrome","Third nerve palsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Variability through the day and fatigability are the discriminators. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing diabetes has a third nerve palsy with a normal, reactive pupil. What is the most common cause?","choices":["Migraine","Microvascular disease","Posterior communicating artery aneurysm","Uncal herniation","Severe head trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and pupil sparing is what makes it the likely one here. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
